          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-3d842b8be44c455a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Keith’s “I love you, Nikki” primarily avows his continuing loving state. Its placement within an apology and plea for contact gives it slight commitment-like relational force, but the words do not clearly undertake a new obligation. Concern and emotional delivery do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance may weakly invoke Keith’s commitment to their relationship, but its clearest core force is truth-conditional avowal and reassurance.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Keith originally speaks the words in a recorded voicemail; the passage directly quotes them as Nikki plays the message.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Keith leaves the declaration after discovering that Nikki has departed without explanation. Surrounded by concern, apology, and “Don’t shut me out,” it reassures her of his feelings and appeals for renewed communication. Nikki’s narration treats his love as real and reciprocal, while questioning whether love can overcome the relationship’s surrounding turmoil. The described emotional weight concerns delivery and reception rather than supplying independent E force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“If I did something to upset you, I’m sorry. I love you, Nikki. Don’t shut me out.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit affirmative declaration strongly supports T; the apology and plea provide limited support for relational or commitment-like P force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Keith says he woke to find Nikki gone, asks her to call, and says he needs to know she is okay.\",\"supports\":\"Frames the declaration as a concerned avowal and reassurance in an attempt to restore communication.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“She loved Keith too, but ... made her question whether love was enough.”\",\"supports\":\"Nikki interprets the message as expressing actual reciprocal love rather than as a semantically empty token or non-love-related use.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Keith’s voice is described as “heavy with concern.”\",\"supports\":\"Establishes emotional delivery but, without evidence of reflex-like production, does not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as an affirmative present-form explicit I–you love expression and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted core event is the ordinary explicit love declaration rather than an extraction error.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the central avowal, with slight P support for its appeal to the existing relationship. No important core force remains outside T/P/E; the surrounding distress and narrative complications are contextual.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
